1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,362
Williams Obstetrics.

2
00:00:02,312 --> 00:00:05,150
Chapter 2: Maternal Anatomy.

3
00:00:05,150 --> 00:00:18,037
Anterior Abdominal Wall Perineum Internal Generative Organs Lower Urinary Tract Pelvic Skeletal Anatomy References Anterior Abdominal Wall # Skin, Subcutaneous Layer, and Fascia.

4
00:00:18,037 --> 00:00:29,187
The anterior abdominal wall confines abdominal viscera, stretches to accommodate the expanding uterus, and provides surgical access to the internal reproductive organs.

5
00:00:29,187 --> 00:00:36,112
Thus, a comprehensive knowledge of its layered structure is required to surgically enter the peritoneal cavity.

6
00:00:36,112 --> 00:00:41,062
Langer lines describe the orientation of dermal fibers within the skin.

7
00:00:41,062 --> 00:00:45,262
In the anterior abdominal wall, they lie transversely.

8
00:00:45,262 --> 00:00:52,400
As a result, vertical skin incisions sustain greater lateral tension and in general develop wider scars.

9
00:00:52,400 --> 00:01:00,912
In contrast, low transverse incisions, such as the Pfannenstiel, follow Langer lines and lead to superior cosmetic results.

10
00:01:00,912 --> 00:01:11,062
The subcutaneous layer can be separated into a superficial, predominantly fatty layer—Camper fascia—and a deeper membranous layer —Scarpa fascia.

11
00:01:11,062 --> 00:01:21,037
Camper fascia continues onto the perineum to provide fatty substance to the mons pubis and labia majora and then to blend with the fat of the ischioanal fossa.

12
00:01:21,037 --> 00:01:27,375
Scarpa fascia continues inferiorly onto the perineum as Colles fascia, (p. 18).

13
00:01:27,375 --> 00:01:42,875
Beneath the subcutaneous layer, the anterior abdominal wall muscles are the midline rectus abdominis and pyramidalis muscles as well as the external oblique, internal oblique, and transversus abdominis muscles, which extend across the entire wall .

14
00:01:42,875 --> 00:01:50,012
The fibrous aponeuroses of these three latter muscles form the primary fascia of the anterior abdominal wall.

15
00:01:50,012 --> 00:02:04,137
These fuse in the midline at the linea alba, which normally sonographically measures ≤15 mm wide below the umbilicus in nongravid women (Beer, 2009; Mota, 2018).

16
00:02:04,137 --> 00:02:09,987
An abnormally wide separation may reflect diastasis recti or ventral hernia.

17
00:02:09,987 --> 00:02:12,225
Figure 2-1.

18
00:02:12,225 --> 00:02:15,150
Anterior abdominal wall anatomy.

19
00:02:15,150 --> 00:02:19,062
Modified with permission from Corton MM: Anatomy.

20
00:02:19,062 --> 00:02:26,650
In Hoffman BL, Schorge JO, Halvorson LM, et al (eds): Williams Gynecology, 4th ed.

21
00:02:26,650 --> 00:02:30,575
New York, NY: McGraw Hill; 2020.

22
00:02:30,575 --> 00:02:36,412
These three aponeuroses also invest the rectus abdominis muscle as the rectus sheath.

23
00:02:36,412 --> 00:02:43,837
The construction of this sheath varies above and below a boundary, termed the arcuate line (see Fig. 2-1).

24
00:02:43,837 --> 00:02:51,612
Cephalad to this border, the aponeuroses invest the rectus abdominis bellies on both dorsal and ventral surfaces.

25
00:02:51,612 --> 00:03:03,000
Caudal to this line, all aponeuroses lie ventral or superficial to the rectus abdominis muscle, and only the thin transversalis fascia and peritoneum lie deep to the rectus .

26
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:10,587
This transition of rectus sheath composition can be seen best in the upper third of a midline vertical abdominal incision.

27
00:03:10,587 --> 00:03:17,750
The paired small triangular pyramidalis muscles originate from the pubic crest and insert into the linea alba.

28
00:03:17,750 --> 00:03:23,637
These muscles lie atop the rectus abdominis muscle but beneath the anterior rectus sheath.

29
00:03:23,637 --> 00:03:31,087
The umbilicus is covered by peritoneum, transversalis fascia, and skin and contains the umbilical ring.

30
00:03:31,087 --> 00:03:37,200
The ring is a defect in the linea alba through which the fetal umbilical vessels previously passed.

31
00:03:37,200 --> 00:03:44,412
The round ligament of the liver and the median umbilical and medial umbilical ligaments variably attach to the ring.

32
00:03:44,412 --> 00:03:48,250
# Transversalis Fascia and Peritoneum.

33
00:03:48,250 --> 00:03:57,012
Transversalis fascia is the thin fibrous tissue layer between the inner surface of the transversus abdominis muscle and the preperitoneal fat.

34
00:03:57,012 --> 00:04:03,187
Inferiorly, the transversalis fascia blends with the periosteum of the pubic bones.

35
00:04:03,187 --> 00:04:09,200
The peritoneum contains a single layer of epithelial cells and a supporting connective tissue.

36
00:04:09,200 --> 00:04:18,850
The visceral peritoneum densely wraps around the abdominopelvic viscera, whereas the parietal peritoneum lines the inner surface of the abdominal walls.

37
00:04:18,850 --> 00:04:28,187
In the anterior abdominal wall, five elevations of parietal peritoneum converge toward the umbilicus and are known as umbilical ligaments.

38
00:04:28,187 --> 00:04:37,012
The single median umbilical ligament is formed by the urachus, a fibrous fetal remnant that extends from the bladder apex to the umbilicus.

39
00:04:37,012 --> 00:04:43,687
Clinically, this ligament may be seen at laparotomy during peritoneal incision as a white, midline,

40
00:04:43,852 --> 00:04:45,739
fibrous cord.

41
00:04:45,689 --> 00:04:51,752
The paired medial umbilical ligaments are formed by the obliterated fetal umbilical arteries.

42
00:04:51,752 --> 00:04:58,952
The paired lateral umbilical ligaments contain the patent inferior epigastric vessels (see Fig. 21).

43
00:04:58,952 --> 00:05:02,052
# Blood Supply and Lymphatics.

44
00:05:02,052 --> 00:05:15,602
The superficial epigastric, superficial circumflex iliac, and superficial external pudendal arteries arise from the femoral artery just below the inguinal ligament within the femoral triangle (see Fig. 2-1).

45
00:05:15,602 --> 00:05:22,627
These vessels supply the skin and subcutaneous layers of the anterior abdominal wall and mons pubis.

46
00:05:22,627 --> 00:05:30,089
The superficial epigastric vessels are surgically important and course diagonally from their origin toward the umbilicus.

47
00:05:30,089 --> 00:05:38,964
With a low transverse skin incision, these vessels can usually be identified at a depth halfway between the skin and the anterior rectus sheath.

48
00:05:38,964 --> 00:05:43,764
They lie above the Scarpa fascia and several centimeters from the midline.

49
00:05:43,764 --> 00:05:48,314
Ideally, these vessels are identified and surgically occluded.

50
00:05:48,314 --> 00:05:52,614
Lymphatics run cephalad–caudad on the lower abdomen .

51
00:05:52,614 --> 00:05:59,302
Most channels lie in the dermis, and a smaller density is found between the Camper and Scarpa fascias .

52
00:05:59,302 --> 00:06:11,714
More deeply, the bilateral inferior epigastric artery and vein are respective branches of the external iliac vessels and supply anterior abdominal wall muscles, nerves, and fascia.

53
00:06:11,714 --> 00:06:21,602
Of surgical relevance, the inferior epigastric vessels initially course lateral to and then posterior to the rectus abdominis muscles, which they supply.

54
00:06:21,602 --> 00:06:25,427
A deep system of lymphatics follows these arteries .

55
00:06:25,427 --> 00:06:34,589
Clinically, when a Maylard incision is used for cesarean delivery, the inferior epigastric vessels may be lacerated during muscle transection.

56
00:06:34,589 --> 00:06:39,289
Preventively, identification and surgical occlusion are preferable.

57
00:06:39,289 --> 00:06:50,614
These vessels rarely may rupture spontaneously or traumatically during pregnancy to create a rectus sheath hematoma (Eckhoff, 2016; Gibbs, 2016).

58
00:06:50,614 --> 00:07:05,877
On each side of the lower anterior abdominal wall, the Hesselbach triangle is the region bounded laterally by the inferior epigastric vessels, inferiorly by the inguinal ligament, and medially by the lateral border of the rectus abdominis muscle.

59
00:07:05,877 --> 00:07:13,239
Hernias that protrude through the abdominal wall in the boundaries of the Hesselbach triangle are termed direct inguinal hernias.

60
00:07:13,239 --> 00:07:22,102
In contrast, indirect inguinal hernias bulge through the deep inguinal ring, which lies lateral to this triangle, and enter the inguinal canal.

61
00:07:22,102 --> 00:07:26,627
Here, contents may exit out the superficial inguinal ring.

62
00:07:26,627 --> 00:07:28,889
# Innervation.

63
00:07:28,889 --> 00:07:41,414
The entire anterior abdominal wall is innervated by intercostal nerves (T7– 11), the subcostal nerve (T12), and the iliohypogastric and the ilioinguinal nerves (L1).

64
00:07:41,414 --> 00:07:54,214
The intercostal and subcostal nerves are anterior rami of the thoracic spinal nerves and run along the lateral and then anterior abdominal wall between the transversus abdominis and internal oblique muscles .

65
00:07:54,214 --> 00:08:01,502
This space, termed the transversus abdominis plane, can be used for postcesarean analgesia blockade .

66
00:08:01,502 --> 00:08:03,639
Figure 2-2.

67
00:08:03,639 --> 00:08:08,839
Intercostal and subcostal nerves are the anterior rami of spinal nerves.

68
00:08:08,839 --> 00:08:16,489
In this figure, an intercostal nerve extends ventrally between the transversus abdominis and internal oblique muscles.

69
00:08:16,489 --> 00:08:25,052
During this path, the nerve gives rise to lateral and anterior cutaneous branches, which innervate the anterior abdominal wall.

70
00:08:25,052 --> 00:08:32,389
As shown by the inserted needle, the transversus abdominis plane (TAP) block takes advantage of this anatomy.

71
00:08:32,389 --> 00:08:37,939
Modified with permission from Hawkins JL: Anesthesia for the pregnant woman.

72
00:08:37,939 --> 00:08:46,914
In Yeomans ER, Hoffman BL, Gilstrap LC III, et al: Cunningham and Gilstraps’s Operative Obstetrics, 3rd ed.

73
00:08:46,914 --> 00:08:51,402
New York, NY: McGraw Hill; 2017.

74
00:08:51,402 --> 00:09:04,927
Near the rectus abdominis lateral borders, anterior branches of the intercostal and subcostal nerves travel superficially to pierce the posterior sheath, rectus muscle, and then anterior sheath to reach the skin.

75
00:09:04,927 --> 00:09:16,214
Thus, these nerve branches may be severed during a Pfannenstiel incision creation during the step in which the overlying anterior rectus sheath is separated from the rectus abdominis muscle .

76
00:09:16,214 --> 00:09:22,364
The iliohypogastric and ilioinguinal nerves originate from the anterior ramus of the first

77
00:09:22,516 --> 00:09:24,691
lumbar spinal nerve.

78
00:09:24,641 --> 00:09:32,991
They emerge lateral to the psoas major muscle and travel across the quadratus lumborum in the retroperitoneum and toward the iliac crest.

79
00:09:32,991 --> 00:09:39,528
Near this crest, both nerves pierce the transversus abdominis muscle and course ventromedially.

80
00:09:39,528 --> 00:09:50,453
At a site 2 to 3 cm medial to the anterior superior iliac spine, the nerves then pierce the internal oblique muscle and course superficial to it toward the midline .

81
00:09:50,453 --> 00:10:01,541
The iliohypogastric nerve perforates the external oblique aponeurosis near the lateral rectus border to provide sensation to the suprapubic skin (see Fig. 2-1).

82
00:10:01,541 --> 00:10:13,303
The ilioinguinal nerve in its course medially travels through the inguinal canal and exits through the superficial inguinal ring, which forms by splitting of external abdominal oblique aponeurosis fibers.

83
00:10:13,303 --> 00:10:19,853
This nerve supplies the skin of the mons pubis, upper labia majora, and medial upper thighs.

84
00:10:19,853 --> 00:10:32,453
The ilioinguinal and iliohypogastric nerves can be severed during a low transverse incision or entrapped during closure, especially if incisions extend beyond the lateral borders of the rectus abdominis muscle .

85
00:10:32,453 --> 00:10:39,903
These nerves carry sensory information only, and injury leads to loss of sensation within the areas supplied.

86
00:10:39,903 --> 00:10:42,941
Rarely, chronic pain may develop .

87
00:10:42,941 --> 00:10:47,316
The T10 dermatome approximates the level of the umbilicus.

88
00:10:47,316 --> 00:10:52,016
Analgesia to this level is suitable for labor and vaginal birth.

89
00:10:52,016 --> 00:10:59,278
Regional analgesia for cesarean delivery or for puerperal sterilization ideally extends to T4.

90
00:10:59,278 --> 00:11:00,891
Perineum.

91
00:11:00,891 --> 00:11:07,516
This diamond-shaped area has boundaries that mirror those of the bony pelvic outlet (p. 28).

92
00:11:07,516 --> 00:11:19,441
These are the pubic symphysis anteriorly, ischiopubic rami and ischial tuberosities anterolaterally, sacrotuberous ligaments posterolaterally, and coccyx posteriorly.

93
00:11:19,441 --> 00:11:31,753
An arbitrary line joining the ischial tuberosities divides the perineum into an anterior triangle, also called the urogenital triangle, and a posterior triangle, termed the anal triangle .

94
00:11:31,753 --> 00:11:34,078
Figure 2-3.

95
00:11:34,078 --> 00:11:38,916
Vulvar structures and subcutaneous layer of urogenital triangle.

96
00:11:38,916 --> 00:11:43,378
Inset: Vestibule boundaries and openings onto the vestibule.

97
00:11:43,378 --> 00:11:47,291
Modified with permission from Corton MM: Anatomy.

98
00:11:47,291 --> 00:11:54,878
In Hoffman BL, Schorge JO, Halvorson LM, et al (eds): Williams Gynecology, 4th ed.

99
00:11:54,878 --> 00:11:58,803
New York, NY: McGraw Hill; 2020.

100
00:11:58,803 --> 00:12:00,803
# Vulva.

101
00:12:00,803 --> 00:12:08,753
Mons Pubis, Labia, and Clitoris The vulva includes all structures visible externally in the urogenital triangle.

102
00:12:08,753 --> 00:12:23,966
These are the mons pubis, labia majora, labia minora, clitoris, hymen, vestibule, urethral opening, greater vestibular (Bartholin) glands, minor vestibular glands, and paraurethral glands (see Fig. 2-3).

103
00:12:23,966 --> 00:12:30,328
The vulva receives innervations and vascular support from the pudendal nerve (p. 22).

104
00:12:30,328 --> 00:12:35,228
The mons pubis is a fat-filled cushion overlying the symphysis pubis.

105
00:12:35,228 --> 00:12:44,878
After puberty, the mons pubis skin is covered by hair that forms the triangular escutcheon, whose base aligns with the upper margin of the symphysis pubis.

106
00:12:44,878 --> 00:12:53,628
In men and some hirsute women, the escutcheon extends farther onto the anterior abdominal wall toward the umbilicus and thus is diamond shaped.

107
00:12:53,628 --> 00:13:02,728
Labia majora usually are 7 to 8 cm long, 2 to 3 cm wide, and 1 to 1.5 cm thick.

108
00:13:02,728 --> 00:13:10,091
They are continuous directly with the mons pubis superiorly, and the round ligaments terminate at their upper borders.

109
00:13:10,091 --> 00:13:16,228
Hair covers the labia majora, and apocrine, eccrine, and sebaceous glands are abundant.

110
00:13:16,228 --> 00:13:24,166
Beneath the skin, a dense connective tissue layer is nearly void of muscular elements but is rich in elastic fibers and fat.

111
00:13:24,166 --> 00:13:30,278
This fat mass provides bulk to the labia majora and is supplied with a rich venous plexus.

112
00:13:30,278 --> 00:13:36,903
During pregnancy, this plexus may develop varicosities from pressure created by the growing uterus.

113
00:13:36,903 --> 00:13:42,466
Each labium minus is a thin tissue fold that lies medial to each labium majus.

114
00:13:42,466 --> 00:13:49,003
The labia minora begin at the fourchette and extend superiorly, where each divides into two lamellae.

115
00:13:49,003 --> 00:13:58,678
From each side, the lower lamellae fuse to form the frenulum of the clitoris, and the upper lamellae merge to form the prepuce (see Fig. 2-3).

116
00:13:58,678 --> 00:14:07,903
The labia minora dimensions vary greatly among reproductive-aged women (Cao, 2015; Lykkebo, 2017).

117
00:14:07,903 --> 00:14:15,066
In one large study, the mean length was 4 cm (range 0.5 to 10 cm)

118
00:14:15,220 --> 00:14:21,332
and mean lateral span was 1.5 (range 0.1 to 6 cm) .

119
00:14:21,282 --> 00:14:30,357
Structurally, the labia minora are composed of connective tissue with many small vessels, elastin fibers, but very little smooth muscle.

120
00:14:30,357 --> 00:14:36,582
Nerve fibers are numerous (Ginger, 2011a; Schober, 2015).

121
00:14:36,582 --> 00:14:40,645
The epithelia of the labia minora differ by location.

122
00:14:40,645 --> 00:14:46,895
Thinly keratinized stratified squamous epithelium covers the outer surface of each labium.

123
00:14:46,895 --> 00:14:54,895
On their inner surface, the lateral portion is covered by this same epithelium up to a demarcating line, termed the Hart line.

124
00:14:54,895 --> 00:15:01,320
Medial to this line, each labium is covered by squamous epithelium that is nonkeratinized.

125
00:15:01,320 --> 00:15:08,545
Labia minora lack hair follicles, eccrine glands, and apocrine glands but have many sebaceous glands .

126
00:15:08,545 --> 00:15:12,395
The clitoris is the principal female erogenous organ .

127
00:15:12,395 --> 00:15:19,970
It is located beneath the prepuce, above the frenulum and urethra, and projects downward toward the vaginal opening.

128
00:15:19,970 --> 00:15:27,757
The clitoris rarely exceeds 2 cm in length and is composed of a glans, a corpus or body, and two crura.

129
00:15:27,757 --> 00:15:35,532
The glans is usually less than 0.5 cm in diameter and is covered by stratified squamous epithelium.

130
00:15:35,532 --> 00:15:40,782
Nerve bundles are prominent and correspond to the paired dorsal nerves of the clitoris .

131
00:15:40,782 --> 00:15:44,557
The clitoral body contains two corpora cavernosa.

132
00:15:44,557 --> 00:15:51,832
Extending from the clitoral body, each corpus cavernosum diverges laterally to form a long, narrow crus.

133
00:15:51,832 --> 00:15:59,620
Each crus lies along the inferior surface of its respective ischiopubic ramus and deep to the ischiocavernosus muscle.

134
00:15:59,620 --> 00:16:04,620
The clitoral blood supply stems from branches of the internal pudendal artery.

135
00:16:04,620 --> 00:16:13,582
Specifically, the deep artery of the clitoris supplies the clitoral body, whereas the dorsal artery of the clitoris supplies the glans and prepuce.

136
00:16:13,582 --> 00:16:15,882
Figure 2-4.

137
00:16:15,882 --> 00:16:21,745
Clitoris and associated vulvar structures in superficial space of urogenital triangle.

138
00:16:21,745 --> 00:16:25,982
Inset: cross section through proximal body of clitoris.

139
00:16:25,982 --> 00:16:30,945
PM = perineal membrane; PS = pubic symphysis.

140
00:16:30,945 --> 00:16:34,857
Modified with permission from Corton MM: Anatomy.

141
00:16:34,857 --> 00:16:42,445
In Hoffman BL, Schorge JO, Halvorson LM, et al (eds): Williams Gynecology, 4th ed.

142
00:16:42,445 --> 00:16:46,370
New York, NY: McGraw Hill; 2020.

143
00:16:46,370 --> 00:17:00,145
Vestibule In adult women, the vestibule is an almond-shaped area that is enclosed by the Hart line laterally, the hymen medially, the clitoral frenulum anteriorly, and the fourchette posteriorly (see Fig. 2-3).

144
00:17:00,145 --> 00:17:13,182
The vestibule is usually perforated by six openings: the urethra, the vagina, two greater vestibular (Bartholin) gland ducts, and ducts of the two largest paraurethral glands—the Skene glands.

145
00:17:13,182 --> 00:17:19,907
The posterior portion of the vestibule between the fourchette and the vaginal opening is called the vestibular fossa .

146
00:17:19,907 --> 00:17:23,220
It is usually observed only in nulliparas.

147
00:17:23,220 --> 00:17:28,957
The bilateral Bartholin glands are 0.5 to 1 cm in diameter.

148
00:17:28,957 --> 00:17:40,382
On their respective side, each lies inferior to the vestibular bulb (bulb of vestibule) and deep to the inferior end of the bulbospongiosus muscle (former bulbocavernosus muscle).

149
00:17:40,382 --> 00:17:49,220
A duct extends medially from each gland, measures 1.5 to 2 cm long, and opens distal to the hymeneal ring.

150
00:17:49,220 --> 00:17:53,807
One duct opens at 5 and the other at 7 o’clock on the vestibule.

151
00:17:53,807 --> 00:18:01,332
Following trauma or infection, either duct may swell and obstruct to form a cyst or, if infected, an abscess.

152
00:18:01,332 --> 00:18:10,045
In contrast, the minor vestibular glands are shallow glands lined by simple mucin-secreting epithelium and open along the Hart line.

153
00:18:10,045 --> 00:18:24,332
The paraurethral glands are a collective arborization of glands whose numerous small ducts open into the urethra and predominantly along its entire inferior aspect (Costa, 2016; Huffman, 1948).

154
00:18:24,332 --> 00:18:31,182
The two largest are called Skene glands, and their ducts typically lie distally and near the urethral meatus.

155
00:18:31,182 --> 00:18:42,482
Clinically, inflammation and duct obstruction of any of the paraurethral glands can lead to urethral diverticulum formation (Chap 56, p. 1007).

156
00:18:42,482 --> 00:18:52,920
The urethral opening or meatus is in the midline of the vestibule, 1 to 1.5 cm below the pubic arch, and a short distance above the vaginal opening.

157
00:18:52,920 --> 00:18:55,582
# Vagina and Hymen.

158
00:18:55,582 --> 00:19:03,482
In adult women, the hymen is a thin membrane that surrounds all or much of the vaginal opening in an annular or crescent form.

159
00:19:03,482 --> 00:19:08,832
Mainly elastic fibers, collagen, and fine vessels compose the hymen.

160
00:19:08,832 --> 00:19:10,320
Nerve

161
00:19:10,468 --> 00:19:13,780
fibers are few and localize to its base.

162
00:19:13,730 --> 00:19:20,705
Both outer and inner hymeneal surfaces are covered by nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium .

163
00:19:20,705 --> 00:19:28,243
The aperture of the intact hymen ranges in diameter from pinpoint to one that admits one or even two fingertips.

164
00:19:28,243 --> 00:19:33,093
As a rule, the hymen is torn at several sites during first coitus.

165
00:19:33,093 --> 00:19:40,655
However, identical tears may form by other penetration, for example, by tampons used during menstruation.

166
00:19:40,655 --> 00:19:43,930
The torn edges soon reepithelialize.

167
00:19:43,930 --> 00:19:52,843
Proximal to the hymen, the vagina is a muscular tube that extends to the uterus and is interposed lengthwise between the bladder and the rectum .

168
00:19:52,843 --> 00:20:00,780
Total vaginal length is 9 to 10 cm (Collins, 2017; Patnam, 2019).

169
00:20:00,780 --> 00:20:08,455
Anteriorly, the vagina is separated from the bladder and urethra by connective tissue—the vesicovaginal septum.

170
00:20:08,455 --> 00:20:16,568
Posteriorly, between the lower portion of the vagina and the rectum, similar tissues together form the rectovaginal septum.

171
00:20:16,568 --> 00:20:24,830
The upper fourth of the vagina is separated from the rectum by the rectouterine pouch, also called the cul-de-sac or pouch of Douglas .

172
00:20:24,830 --> 00:20:27,218
Figure 2-5.

173
00:20:27,218 --> 00:20:30,043
Vagina and surrounding anatomy.

174
00:20:30,043 --> 00:20:33,955
Modified with permission from Corton MM: Anatomy.

175
00:20:33,955 --> 00:20:41,543
In Hoffman BL, Schorge JO, Halvorson LM, et al (eds): Williams Gynecology, 4th ed.

176
00:20:41,543 --> 00:20:45,468
New York, NY: McGraw Hill; 2020.

177
00:20:45,468 --> 00:20:51,243
Normally, the anterior and posterior walls of the vaginal lumen lie in contact.

178
00:20:51,243 --> 00:20:58,655
The vaginal apex is subdivided by the cervix into anterior, posterior, and two lateral fornices.

179
00:20:58,655 --> 00:21:05,230
Clinically, the internal pelvic organs usually can be palpated through the thin walls of these fornices.

180
00:21:05,230 --> 00:21:12,805
The vaginal lining is composed of nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium and underlying lamina propria.

181
00:21:12,805 --> 00:21:19,680
In premenopausal women, this lining is thrown into numerous thin transverse ridges, known as rugae.

182
00:21:19,680 --> 00:21:25,593
Deep to this, a muscular layer contains smooth muscle, collagen, and elastin.

183
00:21:25,593 --> 00:21:34,618
Beneath this, an adventitial layer consists of collagen and elastin (Maldonado, 2020; Mazloomdoost, 2017).

184
00:21:34,618 --> 00:21:39,493
No true fascia separates the vagina from the bladder or from the rectum.

185
00:21:39,493 --> 00:21:41,955
The vagina lacks glands.

186
00:21:41,955 --> 00:21:51,455
Instead, it is lubricated by a transudate that originates from the vaginal subepithelial capillary plexus and crosses the permeable epithelium .

187
00:21:51,455 --> 00:21:57,605
Due to increased vascularity during pregnancy, vaginal secretions are notably increased.

188
00:21:57,605 --> 00:22:08,030
At times, this may be confused with amnionic fluid leakage, and clinical differentiation of these two is described in Chapter 22 (p. 426).

189
00:22:08,030 --> 00:22:16,930
After birth-related epithelial trauma and healing, fragments of stratified epithelium occasionally are embedded beneath the vaginal surface.

190
00:22:16,930 --> 00:22:23,293
Similar to its native tissue, this buried epithelium continues to shed degenerated cells.

191
00:22:23,293 --> 00:22:29,343
As a result, epidermal inclusion cysts, which are filled with debris, may commonly form.

192
00:22:29,343 --> 00:22:32,818
The vagina has an abundant vascular supply.

193
00:22:32,818 --> 00:22:39,218
The proximal portion is supplied by the cervical branch of the uterine artery and by the vaginal artery.

194
00:22:39,218 --> 00:22:46,768
The latter may variably arise from the uterine or inferior vesical artery or directly from the internal iliac artery.

195
00:22:46,768 --> 00:22:56,230
The middle rectal artery contributes supply to the posterior vaginal wall, whereas the distal walls receive contributions from the internal pudendal artery.

196
00:22:56,230 --> 00:23:06,293
At each level, vessels supplying each side of the vagina course medially across the anterior or posterior vaginal wall and form midline anastomoses.

197
00:23:06,293 --> 00:23:12,343
An extensive venous plexus also surrounds the vagina and follows the course of the arteries.

198
00:23:12,343 --> 00:23:19,468
Lymphatics from the lower third, along with those of the vulva, drain primarily into the inguinal lymph nodes.

199
00:23:19,468 --> 00:23:23,380
Those from the remainder drain into the pelvic lymph nodes.

200
00:23:23,380 --> 00:23:25,843
# Perineal Body.

201
00:23:25,843 --> 00:23:39,130
This fibromuscular pyramidal mass lies in the midline at the junction between the urogenital and anal triangles (Figs. 2-3 and 2-5) (Oh, 1973; Soga, 2007).

202
00:23:39,130 --> 00:23:56,568
Clinically, it measures 3.5 to 5 cm in nulliparas from the posterior midline hymen to the mid-anal opening, which are standard pelvic organ prolapse-quantification (POP-Q) landmarks (Komorowski, 2014; Reimers, 2016).

203
00:23:56,568 --> 00:24:05,155
It lengthens slightly during pregnancy, and during secondstage labor, one study showed that it stretches >65 percent .

204
00:24:05,155 --> 00:24:07,093
It serves as

205
00:24:07,252 --> 00:24:12,552
the junction for several structures and provides significant perineal support .

206
00:24:12,502 --> 00:24:21,652
Superficially, the bulbospongiosus, superficial transverse perineal, and external anal sphincter muscles converge on the perineal body.

207
00:24:21,652 --> 00:24:29,189
More deeply, the perineal membrane, portions of the pubococcygeus muscle, and internal anal sphincter contribute .

208
00:24:29,189 --> 00:24:37,964
The perineal body is incised during episiotomy incision and is torn with second-, third-, and fourth-degree lacerations.

209
00:24:37,964 --> 00:24:52,639
Data conflict as to whether a shorter perineal body length predisposes to higher-order lacerations (Deering, 2004; Dua, 2009; Hokenstad, 2015; Meriwether, 2016).

210
00:24:52,639 --> 00:24:55,452
# Urogenital Triangle.

211
00:24:55,452 --> 00:25:07,227
Superficial Space The urogenital triangle is bounded by the pubic rami superiorly, the ischial tuberosities laterally, and the superficial transverse perineal muscles posteriorly .

212
00:25:07,227 --> 00:25:12,102
It is divided into superficial and deep spaces by the perineal membrane.

213
00:25:12,102 --> 00:25:19,802
This membranous partition is a dense fibrous sheet that was previously known as the inferior fascia of the urogenital diaphragm.

214
00:25:19,802 --> 00:25:33,439
The perineal membrane attaches laterally to the ischiopubic rami, medially to the distal third of the urethra and vagina, posteriorly to the perineal body, and anteriorly to the arcuate ligament of the pubis .

215
00:25:33,439 --> 00:25:36,527
It lies at the depth of the hymeneal ring.

216
00:25:36,527 --> 00:25:38,877
Figure 2-6.

217
00:25:38,877 --> 00:25:43,602
Superficial space of the urogenital triangle and anal triangle.

218
00:25:43,602 --> 00:25:49,002
Structures on the left side of the image can be seen after removal of Colles fascia.

219
00:25:49,002 --> 00:25:55,327
Those on the right side are noted after removal of the superficial muscles of the urogenital triangle.

220
00:25:55,327 --> 00:25:59,239
Modified with permission from Corton MM: Anatomy.

221
00:25:59,239 --> 00:26:06,827
In Hoffman BL, Schorge JO, Halvorson LM, et al (eds): Williams Gynecology, 4th ed.

222
00:26:06,827 --> 00:26:10,752
New York, NY: McGraw Hill; 2020.

223
00:26:10,752 --> 00:26:17,689
The superficial space has the perineal membrane as its deep wall and the Colles fascia as its superficial one.

224
00:26:17,689 --> 00:26:24,277
As noted earlier, the Colles fascia is the continuation of the Scarpa fascia onto the perineum.

225
00:26:24,277 --> 00:26:41,452
On the perineum, the Colles fascia securely attaches laterally to the pubic rami and fascia lata of the thigh, inferiorly to the superficial transverse perineal muscle and inferior border of the perineal membrane, and medially to the urethra, clitoris, and vagina.

226
00:26:41,452 --> 00:26:47,889
As such, the superficial space of the urogenital triangle is a relatively closed compartment.

227
00:26:47,889 --> 00:27:05,227
This superficial space contains several important structures, which include the Bartholin glands, vestibular bulbs, clitoral body and crura, branches of the pudendal vessels and nerve, and the ischiocavernosus, bulbospongiosus, and superficial transverse perineal muscles.

228
00:27:05,227 --> 00:27:17,177
Of this muscular trio, the ischiocavernosus muscles each attach on their respective side to the medial aspect of the ischial tuberosity inferiorly and the ischiopubic ramus laterally.

229
00:27:17,177 --> 00:27:25,989
Anteriorly, each attaches to a clitoral crus and may help maintain clitoral erection by compressing the crus to obstruct venous drainage.

230
00:27:25,989 --> 00:27:31,439
Each bulbospongiosus muscle overlies a vestibular bulb and Bartholin gland.

231
00:27:31,439 --> 00:27:37,827
Anteriorly, they attach to the body of the clitoris and posteriorly, to the perineal body.

232
00:27:37,827 --> 00:27:50,152
Some more-recent anatomical studies instead describe that the bulbospongiosus muscles blend medially with the external anal sphincter (Baramee, 2020; Plochocki, 2016).

233
00:27:50,152 --> 00:27:55,702
The muscles constrict the vaginal lumen and aid release of Bartholin gland secretions.

234
00:27:55,702 --> 00:28:01,589
They also may contribute to clitoral erection by compressing the deep dorsal vein of the clitoris.

235
00:28:01,589 --> 00:28:07,402
The bulbospongiosus and ischiocavernosus muscles also pull the clitoris downward.

236
00:28:07,402 --> 00:28:16,927
Last, the superficial transverse perineal muscles are narrow strips that attach to the ischial tuberosities laterally and the perineal body medially.

237
00:28:16,927 --> 00:28:27,289
Each vestibular bulb, also called bulb of vestibule, is an almond-shaped aggregation of veins that lie beneath the bulbospongiosus muscle on either side of the vestibule .

238
00:28:27,289 --> 00:28:31,552
They measure 5 cm long and 2 cm wide .

239
00:28:31,552 --> 00:28:38,777
The bulbs terminate inferiorly at approximately the middle of the vaginal opening and extend upward toward the clitoris.

240
00:28:38,777 --> 00:28:43,839
Their anterior extensions merge in the midline, below the clitoral body.

241
00:28:43,839 --> 00:28:48,939
During childbirth, veins in the vestibular bulb may be lacerated or

242
00:28:49,108 --> 00:28:55,633
may rupture to create a hematoma enclosed within the superficial space of the urogenital triangle.

243
00:28:55,583 --> 00:29:03,808
Deep Space This space lies above or deep to the perineal membrane and below the inferior investing fascia of the levator ani muscle.

244
00:29:03,808 --> 00:29:14,108
It contains portions of urethra and vagina, certain portions of internal pudendal artery branches, and muscles of the striated urogenital sphincter complex .

245
00:29:14,108 --> 00:29:16,408
Figure 2-7.

246
00:29:16,408 --> 00:29:19,395
Deep space of urogenital triangle.

247
00:29:19,395 --> 00:29:25,058
Structures on the right side of the image can be seen after removal of the perineal membrane.

248
00:29:25,058 --> 00:29:37,758
Also shown are structures that attach to the perineal body: bulbospongiosus, superficial transverse perineal, external anal sphincter, and puboperinealis muscles as well as perineal membrane.

249
00:29:37,758 --> 00:29:41,670
Modified with permission from Corton MM: Anatomy.

250
00:29:41,670 --> 00:29:49,258
In Hoffman BL, Schorge JO, Halvorson LM, et al (eds): Williams Gynecology, 4th ed.

251
00:29:49,258 --> 00:29:53,183
New York, NY: McGraw Hill; 2020.

252
00:29:53,183 --> 00:29:54,845
Urethra.

253
00:29:54,845 --> 00:30:05,883
The female urethra measures 3 to 4 cm and originates within the bladder trigone (p. 27) (Hamner, 2018; Rahn, 2007).

254
00:30:05,883 --> 00:30:11,020
The distal two thirds of the urethra are fused with the anterior vaginal wall.

255
00:30:11,020 --> 00:30:17,333
The epithelial lining of the urethra is transitional epithelium at the urethrovesical junction.

256
00:30:17,333 --> 00:30:26,208
It then changes to a pseudostratified columnar along its proximal length but is a nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium distally .

257
00:30:26,208 --> 00:30:33,208
The walls of the urethra consist of two layers of smooth muscle, an inner longitudinal and an outer circular.

258
00:30:33,208 --> 00:30:41,583
This is in turn surrounded by a circular layer of skeletal muscle referred to as the sphincter urethrae (see Fig. 2-7).

259
00:30:41,583 --> 00:30:54,033
Approximately at the junction of the middle and lower third of the urethra and within the deep perineal space, two straplike skeletal muscles called the sphincter urethrovaginalis and the compressor urethrae are found.

260
00:30:54,033 --> 00:31:03,845
Together with the sphincter urethrae, these constitute the external urethral sphincter (EUS), also called the striated urogenital sphincter complex .

261
00:31:03,845 --> 00:31:10,758
The EUS supplies constant tonus and provides emergency reflex contraction to sustain continence.

262
00:31:10,758 --> 00:31:18,995
Distal to the level of the perineal membrane, the walls of the urethra consist of fibrous tissue, serving to direct the urine stream.

263
00:31:18,995 --> 00:31:27,170
Here, the urethra has a prominent submucosal layer that is lined by hormonally sensitive stratified squamous epithelium.

264
00:31:27,170 --> 00:31:35,633
Within the submucosal layer on the dorsal (vaginal) surface of the urethra lie most paraurethral glands (p. 16).

265
00:31:35,633 --> 00:31:43,470
The urethra receives its blood supply from branches of the inferior vesical, vaginal, or internal pudendal arteries.

266
00:31:43,470 --> 00:31:48,033
The pudendal nerve provides somatic innervation to the EUS.

267
00:31:48,033 --> 00:31:58,633
The urethra’s smooth muscle receives sympathetic and parasympathetic innervation from the inferior hypogastric plexus (p. 26) . # Pelvic Diaphragm.

268
00:31:58,633 --> 00:32:06,283
The pelvic diaphragm spans the pelvic outlet and lies deep to the urogenital and anal triangles (p. 17).

269
00:32:06,283 --> 00:32:16,495
This broad muscular floor provides substantial support to the pelvic viscera and is composed of the coccygeus and levator ani muscles (see Fig. 2-7).

270
00:32:16,495 --> 00:32:24,345
The levator ani muscle, in turn, contains the pubococcygeus, puborectalis, and iliococcygeus muscles.

271
00:32:24,345 --> 00:32:32,033
The pubococcygeus muscle is also termed the pubovisceral muscle and is subdivided based on points of insertion and function.

272
00:32:32,033 --> 00:32:42,370
These include the pubovaginalis, puboperinealis, and puboanalis muscles, which insert into the vagina, perineal body, and anus, respectively .

273
00:32:42,370 --> 00:32:51,070
Vaginal birth can damage the levator ani muscle or its innervation (DeLancey, 2003; Weidner, 2006).

274
00:32:51,070 --> 00:33:01,058
Evidence suggests that levator ani trauma may predispose women to later pelvic organ prolapse (Berger, 2018; Dietz, 2008).

275
00:33:01,058 --> 00:33:04,908
Current research efforts aim to minimize these injuries.

276
00:33:04,908 --> 00:33:07,358
# Anal Triangle.

277
00:33:07,358 --> 00:33:13,570
This triangle contains the anal canal, anal sphincter complex, and ischioanal fossae.

278
00:33:13,570 --> 00:33:20,970
The sphincter complex consists of the internal anal sphincter, external anal sphincter, and puborectalis muscle.

279
00:33:20,970 --> 00:33:26,970
The anal triangle also contains branches of the pudendal nerve and internal pudendal vessels.

280
00:33:26,970 --> 00:33:30,920
Anal Canal This is the distal continuation of the rectum.

281
00:33:30,920 --> 00:33:35,033
Canal mucosa is a columnar epithelium proximally.

282
00:33:35,033 --> 00:33:38,720
However, at the pectinate line, also termed

283
00:33:38,884 --> 00:33:45,596
dentate line, simple stratified squamous epithelium begins and continues to the anal verge .

284
00:33:45,546 --> 00:33:53,034
The anal verge is defined as the transition point at which a keratin layer and skin adnexa join the squamous epithelium.

285
00:33:53,034 --> 00:33:56,784
Two definitions describe anal canal boundaries.

286
00:33:56,784 --> 00:34:04,334
The surgical anal canal begins at the levator ani muscle, ends at the anal verge, and measures 4 cm.

287
00:34:04,334 --> 00:34:11,121
The anatomical anal canal lies between the pectineal line and anal verge and is 2 cm long .

288
00:34:11,121 --> 00:34:13,234
Figure 2-8.

289
00:34:13,234 --> 00:34:16,234
Anal canal and ischioanal fossa.

290
00:34:16,234 --> 00:34:20,146
Modified with permission from Corton MM: Anatomy.

291
00:34:20,146 --> 00:34:27,734
In Hoffman BL, Schorge JO, Halvorson LM, et al (eds): Williams Gynecology, 4th ed.

292
00:34:27,734 --> 00:34:31,659
New York, NY: McGraw Hill; 2020.

293
00:34:31,659 --> 00:34:34,871
The anal canal has several tissue layers.

294
00:34:34,871 --> 00:34:45,596
Inner layers include the anal mucosa, the internal anal sphincter, and an intersphincteric space that contains continuation of the rectum’s longitudinal smooth muscle layer.

295
00:34:45,596 --> 00:34:51,971
An outer layer contains the external anal sphincter caudally and the puborectalis muscle cephalad.

296
00:34:51,971 --> 00:35:03,746
Within the anal canal, three highly vascularized submucosal arteriovenous plexuses, termed anal cushions, aid complete closure of the canal and fecal continence when apposed.

297
00:35:03,746 --> 00:35:15,446
Increasing uterine size, excessive straining, and hard stool create raised pressure that ultimately leads to degeneration and subsequent laxity of the cushion’s supportive connective tissue base.

298
00:35:15,446 --> 00:35:19,796
These cushions then protrude into and downward through the anal canal.

299
00:35:19,796 --> 00:35:24,946
This leads to venous engorgement within the cushions—now termed hemorrhoids.

300
00:35:24,946 --> 00:35:31,084
Venous stasis results in inflammation, erosion of the cushion’s epithelium, and bleeding.

301
00:35:31,084 --> 00:35:35,634
External hemorrhoids are those that arise distal to the pectinate line.

302
00:35:35,634 --> 00:35:42,696
They are covered by stratified squamous epithelium and receive sensory innervation from the inferior rectal nerve.

303
00:35:42,696 --> 00:35:47,084
Accordingly, pain and a palpable mass are typical complaints.

304
00:35:47,084 --> 00:35:54,546
Following resolution, a hemorrhoidal tag may remain and is composed of redundant anal skin and fibrotic tissue.

305
00:35:54,546 --> 00:36:02,746
In contrast, internal hemorrhoids are those that form above the pectinate line and are covered by insensitive anorectal mucosa.

306
00:36:02,746 --> 00:36:09,259
These may prolapse or bleed but rarely become painful unless they undergo thrombosis or necrosis.

307
00:36:09,259 --> 00:36:17,834
Anal Sphincter Complex Two sphincters surround the anal canal to provide fecal continence—the external and internal anal sphincters.

308
00:36:17,834 --> 00:36:22,359
Both lie near the vagina and may be torn during vaginal delivery.

309
00:36:22,359 --> 00:36:29,371
The internal anal sphincter (IAS) is a distal continuation of the rectal circular smooth muscle layer.

310
00:36:29,371 --> 00:36:35,759
It receives predominantly parasympathetic fibers, which pass through the pelvic splanchnic nerves.

311
00:36:35,759 --> 00:36:42,534
Along its length, this sphincter is supplied by the superior, middle, and inferior rectal arteries.

312
00:36:42,534 --> 00:36:50,196
The IAS contributes the bulk of anal canal resting pressure for fecal continence and relaxes prior to defecation.

313
00:36:50,196 --> 00:36:59,484
The IAS measures 3 to 4 cm in length, and at its distal margin, it overlaps the external sphincter for 1 to 2 cm .

314
00:36:59,484 --> 00:37:07,046
The distal site at which this overlap ends, called the intersphincteric groove, is palpable on digital examination.

315
00:37:07,046 --> 00:37:09,859
It is 2 to 3 mm thick .

316
00:37:09,859 --> 00:37:22,459
In contrast, the external anal sphincter (EAS) is a striated muscle ring that anteriorly attaches to the perineal body and posteriorly connects to the coccyx via the anococcygeal ligament.

317
00:37:22,459 --> 00:37:33,209
The EAS measures 1.5 to 2.5 cm deep and 6 to 15 mm thick (Fenner, 1998; Stewart, 2018).

318
00:37:33,209 --> 00:37:39,971
The EAS is considered to have one or more subdivisions, although the precise composition is disputed .

319
00:37:39,971 --> 00:37:48,034
Often, a surrounding fibrous capsule is described, but this most likely is perineal body rather than a true EAS sheath .

320
00:37:48,034 --> 00:37:58,384
The EAS maintains a constant resting contraction to aid continence, provides additional squeeze pressure when continence is threatened, yet relaxes for defecation.

321
00:37:58,384 --> 00:38:05,709
The EAS receives blood supply from the inferior rectal artery, which is a branch of the internal pudendal artery.

322
00:38:05,709 --> 00:38:11,784
Somatic motor innervation derives from the inferior rectal branch of the pudendal nerve supply.

323
00:38:11,784 --> 00:38:22,421
Clinically, the IAS and EAS may be involved in third- and fourthdegree lacerations during vaginal delivery, and reunion of these rings is integral to defect

324
00:38:22,588 --> 00:38:24,163
repair .

325
00:38:24,113 --> 00:38:35,250
Ischioanal Fossae These two fat-filled wedge-shaped spaces are found on either side of the anal canal and constitute the bulk of the anal triangle (Figs. 2-8 and 2-9).

326
00:38:35,250 --> 00:38:44,975
Formerly known also as ischiorectal fossae, each fossa has skin as its superficial base, whereas its deep border is formed by the levator ani muscle.

327
00:38:44,975 --> 00:39:03,650
Other borders include: laterally, the obturator internus muscle and ischial tuberosity; medially, the anal sphincter complex and levator ani muscle; posteriorly, the gluteus maximus muscle and sacrotuberous ligament; and anteriorly, the inferior border of the urogenital triangle.

328
00:39:03,650 --> 00:39:05,975
Figure 2-9.

329
00:39:05,975 --> 00:39:08,900
Branches of pudendal nerve and artery.

330
00:39:08,900 --> 00:39:12,813
Modified with permission from Corton MM: Anatomy.

331
00:39:12,813 --> 00:39:20,400
In Hoffman BL, Schorge JO, Halvorson LM, et al (eds): Williams Gynecology, 4th ed.

332
00:39:20,400 --> 00:39:24,325
New York, NY: McGraw Hill; 2020.

333
00:39:24,325 --> 00:39:33,713
The fat found within each fossa provides support to surrounding organs yet allows rectal distention during defecation and vaginal stretching during delivery.

334
00:39:33,713 --> 00:39:40,750
Clinically, injury to vessels in the anal triangle can lead to hematoma formation in the ischioanal fossa.

335
00:39:40,750 --> 00:39:46,375
Moreover, the two fossae communicate posteriorly through the deep postanal space.

336
00:39:46,375 --> 00:39:51,763
This connection lies between the pelvic floor muscles and anococcygeal ligament .

337
00:39:51,763 --> 00:39:58,300
As a result, episiotomy infection or hematoma may extend from one fossa into the other.

338
00:39:58,300 --> 00:40:00,713
# Pudendal Nerve.

339
00:40:00,713 --> 00:40:05,588
This is formed from the anterior rami of S2–4 spinal nerves.

340
00:40:05,588 --> 00:40:17,163
It courses between the piriformis and coccygeus muscles and exits through the greater sciatic foramen at a location posterior to the sacrospinous ligament and just medial to the ischial spine .

341
00:40:17,163 --> 00:40:25,138
Thus, when injecting local anesthetic for a pudendal nerve block, the ischial spine serves as an identifiable landmark .

342
00:40:25,138 --> 00:40:36,488
The pudendal nerve then runs beneath the sacrospinous ligament and above the sacrotuberous ligament as it reenters the lesser sciatic foramen to course across the obturator internus muscle.

343
00:40:36,488 --> 00:40:42,675
Atop this muscle, the nerve lies within the pudendal canal, also known as the Alcock canal.

344
00:40:42,675 --> 00:40:48,038
This space is formed by splitting of the obturator internus investing fascia .

345
00:40:48,038 --> 00:40:56,038
In general, the pudendal nerve is relatively fixed as it courses behind the sacrospinous ligament and within the pudendal canal.

346
00:40:56,038 --> 00:41:03,125
Accordingly, it may be at risk of stretch injury during downward displacement of the pelvic floor during childbirth .

347
00:41:03,125 --> 00:41:11,025
The pudendal nerve leaves this canal to enter the perineum and divides into three terminal branches (see Fig. 2-9).

348
00:41:11,025 --> 00:41:20,113
The first of these, the dorsal nerve of the clitoris, runs between the ischiocavernosus muscle and perineal membrane to supply the clitoral glans .

349
00:41:20,113 --> 00:41:25,038
Second, the perineal nerve runs superficial to the perineal membrane .

350
00:41:25,038 --> 00:41:34,500
It divides into posterior labial branches and muscular branches, which serve the labial skin and the urogenital triangle muscles, respectively.

351
00:41:34,500 --> 00:41:44,125
Last, the inferior rectal branch runs through the ischioanal fossa to supply the external anal sphincter, the anal mucosa, and the perianal skin .

352
00:41:44,125 --> 00:41:52,225
The major blood supply to the perineum is from the internal pudendal artery, and its branches mirror the divisions of the pudendal nerve.

353
00:41:52,225 --> 00:42:00,950
Relevant to pudendal nerve blockade, the internal pudendal artery is the closest vascular structure, within 5 to 8 mm.

354
00:42:00,950 --> 00:42:11,463
It runs deep to the spine in most cases as it exits the greater sciatic foramen (Dueñas-Garcia, 2017; Roshanravan, 2007).

355
00:42:11,463 --> 00:42:13,963
Internal Generative Organs.

356
00:42:13,963 --> 00:42:16,113
# Uterus.

357
00:42:16,113 --> 00:42:23,138
The nonpregnant uterus lies in the pelvic cavity between the bladder anteriorly and the rectum posteriorly.

358
00:42:23,138 --> 00:42:29,625
Anterior and posterior uterine walls are lined by serosa, that is, visceral peritoneum .

359
00:42:29,625 --> 00:42:36,825
This peritoneum continues from the posterior wall to create the rectouterine space (see Fig. 25).

360
00:42:36,825 --> 00:42:41,238
It continues anteriorly to create the vesicouterine pouch.

361
00:42:41,238 --> 00:42:50,663
Clinically, during cesarean delivery, the peritoneum of the vesicouterine pouch is sharply incised, and the vesicocervical space is entered.

362
00:42:50,663 --> 00:42:56,063
This space is a well-defined loose connective tissue layer between the cervix and bladder .

363
00:42:56,063 --> 00:43:02,888
Dissection caudally within this space lifts the bladder safely off the cervix and lower uterine segment for

364
00:43:03,052 --> 00:43:05,502
hysterotomy and delivery .

365
00:43:05,452 --> 00:43:07,702
Figure 2-10.

366
00:43:07,702 --> 00:43:11,564
Uterus, adnexa, and associated anatomy.

367
00:43:11,564 --> 00:43:15,477
Modified with permission from Corton MM: Anatomy.

368
00:43:15,477 --> 00:43:23,064
In Hoffman BL, Schorge JO, Halvorson LM, et al (eds): Williams Gynecology, 4th ed.

369
00:43:23,064 --> 00:43:26,989
New York, NY: McGraw Hill; 2020.

370
00:43:26,989 --> 00:43:32,064
The uterus is pear shaped and consists of two major but unequal parts.

371
00:43:32,064 --> 00:43:39,214
The upper, larger portion is the body or corpus, whereas the lower smaller cervix projects into the vagina.

372
00:43:39,214 --> 00:43:42,352
The isthmus is the union site of these two.

373
00:43:42,352 --> 00:43:48,802
It is of special obstetrical significance because it forms the lower uterine segment during pregnancy.

374
00:43:48,802 --> 00:43:55,427
At each superolateral margin of the body is a uterine cornu, from which a fallopian tube emerges.

375
00:43:55,427 --> 00:44:00,914
This area also contains the origins of the round and uteroovarian ligaments.

376
00:44:00,914 --> 00:44:07,164
Between the points of fallopian tube insertion is the convex upper uterine segment termed the fundus.

377
00:44:07,164 --> 00:44:11,464
The bulk of the uterine body, but not the cervix, is muscle.

378
00:44:11,464 --> 00:44:18,727
The inner surfaces of the uterine walls lie almost in contact, and the intervening cavity forms a mere slit.

379
00:44:18,727 --> 00:44:26,164
The nulligravid uterus measures 6 to 8 cm in length compared with 9 to 10 cm in multiparas.

380
00:44:26,164 --> 00:44:35,277
The uterus averages 60 g and typically weighs more in parous women (Langlois, 1970; Sheikhazadi, 2010).

381
00:44:35,277 --> 00:44:40,777
Pregnancy stimulates remarkable uterine growth due to muscle fiber hypertrophy.

382
00:44:40,777 --> 00:44:47,989
The uterine fundus, a previously flattened convexity between tubal insertions, now becomes dome shaped.

383
00:44:47,989 --> 00:44:54,452
Moreover, the round ligaments appear to insert at the junction of the middle and upper thirds of the organ.

384
00:44:54,452 --> 00:44:59,777
The fallopian tubes elongate, but the ovaries grossly appear unchanged.

385
00:44:59,777 --> 00:45:07,802
Cervix This portion of the uterus is cylindrical and has small apertures at each end —the internal os and external os.

386
00:45:07,802 --> 00:45:12,502
The endocervical canal runs through the cervix and connects these ora.

387
00:45:12,502 --> 00:45:18,914
The cervix is divided into upper and lower portions by the vagina’s attachment to its outer surface.

388
00:45:18,914 --> 00:45:30,677
The upper portion— the portio supravaginalis—begins at the internal os, which corresponds to the level of the vesicouterine peritoneum (see Figs. 2-5 and 2-10).

389
00:45:30,677 --> 00:45:36,077
The lower cervical portion protrudes into the vagina as the portio vaginalis.

390
00:45:36,077 --> 00:45:41,964
Before childbirth, the external cervical os is a small, regular, oval opening.

391
00:45:41,964 --> 00:45:53,014
After labor, especially vaginal childbirth, the orifice is converted into a transverse slit that is divided such that there are the so-called anterior and posterior cervical lips.

392
00:45:53,014 --> 00:46:04,527
If torn deeply during labor or delivery, the cervix may heal in such a manner that it appears irregular, nodular, or stellate (Fig. 36-1, p. 635).

393
00:46:04,527 --> 00:46:14,914
The cervical surface that radially surrounds the external os is called the ectocervix and is lined predominantly by nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium.

394
00:46:14,914 --> 00:46:25,114
In contrast, the endocervical canal is covered by a single layer of mucin-secreting columnar epithelium, which creates deep cleftlike infoldings or “glands.

395
00:46:25,114 --> 00:46:33,877
Commonly during pregnancy, the endocervical epithelium moves out and onto the ectocervix in a physiological process termed eversion .

396
00:46:33,877 --> 00:46:41,639
The cervical stroma is composed mainly of collagen, elastin, and proteoglycans, but very little smooth muscle.

397
00:46:41,639 --> 00:46:52,439
As described in Chapter 21 (p. 406), changes in the amount, composition, and orientation of these components lead to cervical ripening prior to labor.

398
00:46:52,439 --> 00:47:02,814
In early pregnancy, increased vascularity within the cervix stroma beneath the epithelium creates an ectocervical blue tint that is characteristic of the Chadwick sign.

399
00:47:02,814 --> 00:47:09,277
Cervical edema leads to softening—the Goodell sign, whereas isthmic softening is the Hegar sign.

400
00:47:09,277 --> 00:47:19,739
Myometrium and Endometrium Most of the uterus is composed of myometrium, which contains smooth muscle bundles united by connective tissue with many elastic fibers.

401
00:47:19,739 --> 00:47:26,289
Interlacing myometrial fibers surround myometrial vessels and contract to compress these.

402
00:47:26,289 --> 00:47:31,627
This anatomy allows hemostasis at the placental site during third-stage labor.

403
00:47:31,627 --> 00:47:41,477
The uterine cavity is lined with endometrium, which is composed of an overlying epithelium, invaginating glands, and a supportive, vascular stroma.

404
00:47:41,477 --> 00:47:45,777
Throughout the menstrual cycle, the endometrium varies greatly .

405
00:47:45,777 --> 00:47:48,689
It is divided into a functionalis

406
00:47:48,844 --> 00:47:57,306
layer, which is sloughed with menses, and a basalis layer, which serves to regenerate the functionalis layer following each menses.

407
00:47:57,256 --> 00:48:04,581
During pregnancy, the endometrium undergoes dramatic hormonally driven alterations and is termed decidua.

408
00:48:04,581 --> 00:48:15,869
Ligaments Several ligaments extend from the uterine surface toward the pelvic sidewalls and include the round, broad, cardinal, and uterosacral ligaments (see Fig. 2-10).

409
00:48:15,869 --> 00:48:25,756
Despite their appellation, the round and broad ligaments provide no substantial uterine support, which contrasts with the cardinal and uterosacral ligaments.

410
00:48:25,756 --> 00:48:32,081
The round ligament originates somewhat below and anterior to the origin of the fallopian tubes.

411
00:48:32,081 --> 00:48:38,206
Clinically, this orientation can aid tube identification during puerperal sterilization .

412
00:48:38,206 --> 00:48:48,344
Each round ligament extends laterally and down into the inguinal canal, through which it passes, to terminate in the upper portion of the ipsilateral labium majus.

413
00:48:48,344 --> 00:48:56,631
The artery to the round ligament, formerly the Sampson artery, is a small branch of the uterine artery and runs within the ligament.

414
00:48:56,631 --> 00:49:06,781
In nonpregnant women, the round ligament varies from 3 to 5 mm in diameter and is composed of smooth muscle bundles separated by fibrous tissue septa .

415
00:49:06,781 --> 00:49:13,419
During pregnancy, these ligaments hypertrophy and increase appreciably in both length and diameter.

416
00:49:13,419 --> 00:49:21,969
Rare, round ligament varicosities can mimic an inguinal hernia, and color Doppler interrogation of the mass aids diagnosis .

417
00:49:21,969 --> 00:49:29,031
The broad ligaments are two winglike structures that extend from the lateral uterine margins to the pelvic sidewalls.

418
00:49:29,031 --> 00:49:33,594
Each broad ligament consists of a double-layer drape of peritoneum.

419
00:49:33,594 --> 00:49:40,856
The anterior and posterior layers of this drape are termed the anterior and posterior leaves, respectively.

420
00:49:40,856 --> 00:49:47,144
In forming the broad ligament, this peritoneum folds over structures extending from each cornu.

421
00:49:47,144 --> 00:49:58,019
Peritoneum that folds over the fallopian tube is termed the mesosalpinx, that around the round ligament is the mesoteres, and that over the ovarian ligament is the mesovarium.

422
00:49:58,019 --> 00:50:08,931
Peritoneum that extends beneath the fimbriated end of the uterine tube toward the pelvic wall forms infundibulopelvic ligament, which is also the suspensory ligament of the ovary.

423
00:50:08,931 --> 00:50:18,244
This contains nerves and the ovarian vessels, and during pregnancy, these vessels, especially the venous plexuses, are dramatically enlarged.

424
00:50:18,244 --> 00:50:27,831
Specifically, the diameter of the ovarian vascular pedicle increases from 0.9 cm to reach 2.6 cm at term .

425
00:50:27,831 --> 00:50:36,794
The cardinal ligament—formerly the transverse cervical ligament or the Mackenrodt ligament—anchors medially to the uterus and upper vagina.

426
00:50:36,794 --> 00:50:40,694
The cardinal ligament is the thick base of the broad ligament.

427
00:50:40,694 --> 00:50:48,669
As such, during cesarean hysterectomy, sturdy clamps and suture are required for its transection and ligation.

428
00:50:48,669 --> 00:51:03,431
Each uterosacral ligament originates with a posterolateral attachment to the supravaginal portion of the cervix and inserts into the fascia over the sacrum, with some variations (Ramanah, 2012; Umek, 2004).

429
00:51:03,431 --> 00:51:10,144
These ligaments are composed of connective tissue, small neurovascular bundles, and some smooth muscle.

430
00:51:10,144 --> 00:51:16,394
Covered by peritoneum, these ligaments form the lateral boundaries of the rectouterine space.

431
00:51:16,394 --> 00:51:23,306
The term parametrium describes the connective tissues adjacent and lateral to the uterus within the broad ligament.

432
00:51:23,306 --> 00:51:30,869
Paracervical tissues are those adjacent to the cervix, whereas paracolpium is tissue lateral to the vaginal walls.

433
00:51:30,869 --> 00:51:33,644
# Pelvic Blood Supply.

434
00:51:33,644 --> 00:51:43,731
During pregnancy, uterine vasculature, which is supplied principally from the uterine and ovarian arteries, markedly hypertrophies (see Fig. 2-10).

435
00:51:43,731 --> 00:51:52,606
The uterine artery is a main branch of the internal iliac artery (prior hypogastric artery) and enters the base of the broad ligament.

436
00:51:52,606 --> 00:51:58,556
From its origin, the uterine artery courses medially to the lateral side of the uterus.

437
00:51:58,556 --> 00:52:05,144
Approximately 2 cm lateral to the cervix, the uterine artery crosses over the ureter.

438
00:52:05,144 --> 00:52:14,294
This proximity is important surgically, as the ureter may be injured or ligated during hysterectomy when the uterine vessels are clamped and ligated.

439
00:52:14,294 --> 00:52:18,569
Once the uterine artery reaches the cervix, it divides.

440
00:52:18,569 --> 00:52:21,131
The smaller cervicovaginal

441
00:52:21,292 --> 00:52:25,604
artery supplies blood to the lower cervix and upper vagina.

442
00:52:25,554 --> 00:52:33,492
Instead, the main uterine artery branch turns abruptly upward and travels cephalad along the lateral margin of the uterus.

443
00:52:33,492 --> 00:52:38,642
Along this path, the uterine artery gives rise to the arcuate arteries.

444
00:52:38,642 --> 00:52:48,879
Indicated by the name, each branch arches across either the anterior or posterior uterine wall and courses within the myometrium just beneath the serosal surface.

445
00:52:48,879 --> 00:52:53,429
Arcuate vessels from each side anastomose at the uterine midline.

446
00:52:53,429 --> 00:53:00,567
From the arcuate arteries, radial arteries originate at right angles and travel inward through the myometrium.

447
00:53:00,567 --> 00:53:08,042
Upon entering the endometrium/decidua, they branch to become either basal arteries or spiral arteries.

448
00:53:08,042 --> 00:53:12,117
The coiled spiral arteries supply the functionalis layer.

449
00:53:12,117 --> 00:53:18,329
The basal arteries, also called the straight arteries, extend only into the basalis layer.

450
00:53:18,329 --> 00:53:24,379
As the uterine artery courses cephalad, it gives rise to the artery of the round ligament.

451
00:53:24,379 --> 00:53:29,754
At the each cornu, the uterine artery divides into three terminal branches.

452
00:53:29,754 --> 00:53:39,479
First, the tubal branch makes its way through the mesosalpinx and supplies part of the fallopian tube; whereas the fundal branch penetrates the uppermost uterus.

453
00:53:39,479 --> 00:53:46,992
Third, the ovarian branch of the uterine artery forms an anastomosis with the terminal branch of the ovarian artery.

454
00:53:46,992 --> 00:53:54,529
The ovarian artery is a direct branch of the aorta and enters the broad ligament through the infundibulopelvic ligament.

455
00:53:54,529 --> 00:53:59,792
At the ovarian hilum, it divides into smaller branches that enter the ovary.

456
00:53:59,792 --> 00:54:08,154
As the ovarian artery runs along the hilum, it also sends several branches through the mesosalpinx to supply the fallopian tubes.

457
00:54:08,154 --> 00:54:14,729
Its main stem, however, traverses the entire length of the broad ligament toward the uterine cornu.

458
00:54:14,729 --> 00:54:20,142
Here, it forms an anastomosis with the ovarian branch of the uterine artery.

459
00:54:20,142 --> 00:54:31,504
This dual uterine blood supply creates a vascular reserve to prevent uterine ischemia if ligation of the uterine or internal iliac artery is performed to control postpartum hemorrhage.

460
00:54:31,504 --> 00:54:35,179
Uterine veins accompany their respective arteries.

461
00:54:35,179 --> 00:54:43,979
As such, the arcuate veins unite to form the uterine vein, which empties into the internal iliac vein and then the common iliac vein.

462
00:54:43,979 --> 00:54:51,467
Some of the blood from the upper uterus, the ovary, and the upper part of the broad ligament is collected by several veins.

463
00:54:51,467 --> 00:54:54,392
These terminate in the ovarian vein.

464
00:54:54,392 --> 00:54:58,854
From here, the right ovarian vein empties into the vena cava.

465
00:54:58,854 --> 00:55:02,879
The left ovarian vein empties into the left renal vein.

466
00:55:02,879 --> 00:55:08,892
Blood supply to the pelvis is predominantly provided by branches of the internal iliac artery .

467
00:55:08,892 --> 00:55:17,754
These branches are organized into anterior and posterior divisions, and subsequent branches are highly variable between individuals.

468
00:55:17,754 --> 00:55:35,267
The anterior division provides blood supply to the pelvic organs and perineum and includes the inferior gluteal, internal pudendal, middle rectal, vaginal, uterine, and obturator arteries, as well as the umbilical artery and its continuation as the superior vesical artery.

469
00:55:35,267 --> 00:55:44,167
The posterior division branches extend to the buttock and thigh and include the superior gluteal, lateral sacral, and iliolumbar arteries.

470
00:55:44,167 --> 00:55:55,179
For this reason, during internal iliac artery ligation, many advocate ligation distal to the posterior division to avoid compromise to the areas supplied by this division .

471
00:55:55,179 --> 00:55:57,467
Figure 2-11.

472
00:55:57,467 --> 00:55:59,454
Pelvic arteries.

473
00:55:59,454 --> 00:56:03,367
Modified with permission from Corton MM: Anatomy.

474
00:56:03,367 --> 00:56:10,954
In Hoffman BL, Schorge JO, Halvorson LM, et al (eds): Williams Gynecology, 4th ed.

475
00:56:10,954 --> 00:56:14,879
New York, NY: McGraw Hill; 2020.

476
00:56:14,879 --> 00:56:17,567
# Pelvic Lymphatics.

477
00:56:17,567 --> 00:56:23,042
The lymphatic vessels from the uterine corpus generally empty into two nodal groups.

478
00:56:23,042 --> 00:56:30,704
Those from the cervix and lower uterine segment travel to the pelvic lymph nodes, which then drain into the paraaortic nodes.

479
00:56:30,704 --> 00:56:38,267
Vessels from the fundus, after joining lymphatics from the adnexa, directly terminate in the paraaortic lymph nodes.

480
00:56:38,267 --> 00:56:42,504
Vaginal lymphatic channels have extensive anastomoses.

481
00:56:42,504 --> 00:56:49,542
As a result, any node in the pelvis, groin, or anorectal area may drain any part of the vagina.

482
00:56:49,542 --> 00:56:55,842
Lymphatics of the vulva and distal vagina typically empty into the superficial inguinal nodal group.

483
00:56:55,842 --> 00:56:58,604
From here, lymph travels through

484
00:56:58,756 --> 00:57:02,593
the deep femoral lymphatics to the pelvic nodal groups.

485
00:57:02,543 --> 00:57:05,206
# Pelvic Innervation.

486
00:57:05,206 --> 00:57:18,006
As a brief review, the peripheral nervous system is divided into a somatic division, which innervates skeletal muscle, and an autonomic division, which innervates smooth muscle, cardiac muscle, and glands.

487
00:57:18,006 --> 00:57:26,131
Pelvic visceral innervation is predominantly autonomic, which is further divided into sympathetic and parasympathetic components.

488
00:57:26,131 --> 00:57:34,306
Sympathetic innervation to pelvic viscera begins with the superior hypogastric plexus, also termed the presacral nerve .

489
00:57:34,306 --> 00:57:40,768
This plexus is formed by sympathetic fibers arising from spinal levels T10 through L2.

490
00:57:40,768 --> 00:57:52,418
At the level of the sacral promontory, the superior hypogastric plexus divides into a right and a left hypogastric nerve, which run downward along their respective pelvic sidewalls .

491
00:57:52,418 --> 00:57:54,718
Figure 2-12.

492
00:57:54,718 --> 00:57:56,768
Pelvic innervation.

493
00:57:56,768 --> 00:58:00,681
Modified with permission from Corton MM: Anatomy.

494
00:58:00,681 --> 00:58:08,268
In Hoffman BL, Schorge JO, Halvorson LM, et al (eds): Williams Gynecology, 4th ed.

495
00:58:08,268 --> 00:58:12,193
New York, NY: McGraw Hill; 2020.

496
00:58:12,193 --> 00:58:19,893
In contrast, parasympathetic innervation to the pelvic organs derives from spinal levels S2 through S4.

497
00:58:19,893 --> 00:58:29,181
Anterior rami of the spinal nerves for those levels combine on each side to form the pelvic splanchnic nerves, also termed nervi erigentes.

498
00:58:29,181 --> 00:58:41,731
Blending of the two hypogastric nerves (sympathetic) and the two pelvic splanchnic nerves (parasympathetic) gives rise to the inferior hypogastric plexus, also termed the pelvic plexus.

499
00:58:41,731 --> 00:58:52,281
From here, fibers of this plexus extend to the bladder, uterus, upper vagina, and rectum (Ripperda, 2017; Spackman, 2007).

500
00:58:52,281 --> 00:59:00,143
Extensions of the inferior hypogastric plexus also reach the perineum to innervate the clitoris and vestibular bulbs .

501
00:59:00,143 --> 00:59:11,718
For the uterus, most of its afferent sensory fibers ascend through the inferior hypogastric plexus and enter the spinal cord via T10 through T12 and L1 spinal nerves.

502
00:59:11,718 --> 00:59:16,968
These transmit the painful stimuli of contractions to the central nervous system.

503
00:59:16,968 --> 00:59:26,406
For the cervix and upper part of the birth canal, sensory nerves pass through the pelvic splanchnic nerves to the second, third, and fourth sacral nerves.

504
00:59:26,406 --> 00:59:32,293
Last, those from the lower portion of the birth canal pass primarily through the pudendal nerve.

505
00:59:32,293 --> 00:59:37,131
Anesthetic blocks used during delivery target these levels of innervation.

506
00:59:37,131 --> 00:59:39,293
# Ovaries.

507
00:59:39,293 --> 00:59:48,793
Along the pelvic sidewall, each ovary usually rests in the ovarian fossa, which is a slight depression between the external and internal iliac vessels.

508
00:59:48,793 --> 01:00:01,868
During childbearing years, ovaries variably measure 2.5 to 5 cm in length, 1.5 to 3 cm in width, and 0.6 to 1.5 cm in thickness.

509
01:00:01,868 --> 01:00:13,418
The ovarian ligament, also called the uteroovarian ligament, originates from the posterolateral uterine cornu and extends to the uterine pole of the ovary (see Fig. 2-10).

510
01:00:13,418 --> 01:00:24,243
Measuring a few centimeters long and 3 to 4 mm in diameter, this ligament is made up of muscle and connective tissue and is covered by peritoneum—the mesovarium.

511
01:00:24,243 --> 01:00:29,481
Blood supply reaches the ovary through the mesovarium to enter the ovarian hilum.

512
01:00:29,481 --> 01:00:33,668
The ovary consists of an outer cortex and inner medulla.

513
01:00:33,668 --> 01:00:40,318
In young women, the cortex is smooth, white, and lined by a single layer of cuboidal epithelium.

514
01:00:40,318 --> 01:00:46,731
This epithelium is supported by an inner connective tissue condensation, the tunica albuginea.

515
01:00:46,731 --> 01:00:52,381
Beneath this, the ovarian cortex contains oocytes and developing follicles.

516
01:00:52,381 --> 01:00:59,731
The medulla is composed of loose connective tissue, numerous arteries and veins, and a small amount of smooth muscle.

517
01:00:59,731 --> 01:01:04,706
The ovaries are supplied with both sympathetic and parasympathetic nerves.

518
01:01:04,706 --> 01:01:13,368
The sympathetic nerves are derived primarily from the ovarian plexus that accompanies the ovarian vessels and originates in the renal plexus.

519
01:01:13,368 --> 01:01:18,756
Others derive from the plexus that surrounds the ovarian branch of the uterine artery.

520
01:01:18,756 --> 01:01:22,356
Parasympathetic input is from the vagus nerve.

521
01:01:22,356 --> 01:01:28,443
Sensory afferents follow the ovarian artery and enter at T10 spinal cord level.

522
01:01:28,443 --> 01:01:31,056
# Fallopian Tubes.

523
01:01:31,056 --> 01:01:37,956
Each of these serpentine tubes extend laterally 8 to 14 cm from their respective uterine cornu.

524
01:01:37,956 --> 01:01:44,493
Along their length, they contain an interstitial portion, isthmus, ampulla, and infundibulum .

525
01:01:44,493 --> 01:01:48,118
Most proximal, the interstitial portion is

526
01:01:48,268 --> 01:01:51,443
embodied within the uterine muscular wall.

527
01:01:51,393 --> 01:01:59,580
Next, the narrow 2- to 3-mm wide isthmus widens gradually into the 5- to 8-mm wide ampulla.

528
01:01:59,580 --> 01:02:07,455
Last, the infundibulum is the funnel-shaped fimbriated distal extremity of the tube, which opens into the abdominal cavity.

529
01:02:07,455 --> 01:02:16,493
These latter three extrauterine portions are covered by the mesosalpinx, which is a superior extension of the broad ligament and described next.

530
01:02:16,493 --> 01:02:19,068
Figure 2-13.

531
01:02:19,068 --> 01:02:30,405
The fallopian tube of an adult woman with cross-sectioned illustrations of the gross structure in several portions: (A) isthmus, (B) ampulla, and (C) infundibulum.

532
01:02:30,405 --> 01:02:35,068
Below these are photographs of corresponding histological sections.

533
01:02:35,068 --> 01:02:39,180
Reproduced with permission from Dr. Kelley S. Carrick.

534
01:02:39,180 --> 01:02:45,918
In cross section, the uterine tube contains a mesosalpinx, myosalpinx, and endosalpinx.

535
01:02:45,918 --> 01:02:52,993
The outer of these, the mesosalpinx, is a single-cell mesothelial layer functioning as visceral peritoneum.

536
01:02:52,993 --> 01:02:59,393
In the myosalpinx, smooth muscle is arranged in an inner circular and an outer longitudinal layer.

537
01:02:59,393 --> 01:03:07,780
The tubal musculature undergoes rhythmic contractions constantly, the rate of which varies with cyclical ovarian hormonal changes.

538
01:03:07,780 --> 01:03:19,143
The endosalpinx (tubal mucosa) is a single layer of columnar epithelium made up of ciliated, secretory, and intercalary cells resting on a sparse lamina propria.

539
01:03:19,143 --> 01:03:26,930
Clinically, its close proximity to the underlying myosalpinx contributes to easy invasion by ectopic trophoblast.

540
01:03:26,930 --> 01:03:33,868
The tubal mucosa is arranged in longitudinal folds that become progressively more complex toward the fimbria.

541
01:03:33,868 --> 01:03:39,580
In the ampulla, the lumen is occupied almost completely by the arborescent mucosa.

542
01:03:39,580 --> 01:03:44,330
The current produced by the tubal cilia flows toward the uterine cavity.

543
01:03:44,330 --> 01:03:50,630
Tubal peristalsis created by cilia and muscular layer contraction aids ovum transport .

544
01:03:50,630 --> 01:03:56,255
The tubes are supplied richly with elastic tissue, blood vessels, and lymphatics.

545
01:03:56,255 --> 01:04:02,355
Their sympathetic innervation is extensive, in contrast to their parasympathetic innervation.

546
01:04:02,355 --> 01:04:09,505
This nerve supply derives partly from the ovarian plexus and partly from the inferior hypogastric plexus.

547
01:04:09,505 --> 01:04:14,218
Sensory afferent fibers ascend to T10 spinal cord levels.

548
01:04:14,218 --> 01:04:16,580
Lower Urinary Tract.

549
01:04:16,580 --> 01:04:18,555
# Bladder.

550
01:04:18,555 --> 01:04:27,793
Anteriorly, the bladder rests against the inner surface of the pubic bones and then, as it fills, also against the anterior abdominal wall.

551
01:04:27,793 --> 01:04:31,993
Posteriorly, it rests against the vagina and cervix.

552
01:04:31,993 --> 01:04:38,105
The bladder is divided into a dome and a base approximately at the level of the ureteral orifices.

553
01:04:38,105 --> 01:04:44,893
The dome is thin walled and distensible, whereas the base is thicker and undergoes less distention during filling.

554
01:04:44,893 --> 01:04:52,368
The vesical trigone lies in the bladder base and contains both ureteral orifices and the internal urinary meatus.

555
01:04:52,368 --> 01:04:59,093
The urethral lumen begins at this meatus and then courses through the bladder base for less than 1 cm.

556
01:04:59,093 --> 01:05:04,293
This region where the urethral lumen traverses the bladder base is the bladder neck.

557
01:05:04,293 --> 01:05:12,693
The bladder wall consists of coarse bundles of smooth muscle known as the detrusor muscle, which extends into the proximal part of the urethra.

558
01:05:12,693 --> 01:05:17,955
A submucosal layer intervenes between this detrusor muscle and the mucosa.

559
01:05:17,955 --> 01:05:23,993
The bladder mucosa consists of transitional epithelium and underlying lamina propria.

560
01:05:23,993 --> 01:05:35,218
The blood supply to the bladder arises from the superior vesical arteries, which are branches of the patent portion of the umbilical artery and supply the dome (see Fig. 2-11).

561
01:05:35,218 --> 01:05:45,468
The inferior vesical arteries supply the base and variably arise from either the umbilical, uterine, or vaginal artery (de Treigny, 2017).

562
01:05:45,468 --> 01:05:52,293
The nerve supply to the bladder arises from the inferior hypogastric plexus (see Fig. 2-12).

563
01:05:52,293 --> 01:05:54,305
# Ureter.

564
01:05:54,305 --> 01:06:05,018
As the ureter enters the pelvis, it crosses over the bifurcation of the common iliac artery and passes just medial to the ovarian vessels (see Fig. 2-10).

565
01:06:05,018 --> 01:06:14,005
As the ureter descends into the pelvis, it lies medial to the internal iliac branches and anterolateral to the uterosacral ligaments.

566
01:06:14,005 --> 01:06:21,080
The ureter then traverses through the cardinal ligament approximately 1 to 2 cm lateral to the cervix.

567
01:06:21,080 --> 01:06:28,980
Near the level of the uterine isthmus, it courses below the uterine artery and travels anteromedially toward the bladder base.

568
01:06:28,980 --> 01:06:30,555
In this

569
01:06:30,724 --> 01:06:39,411
path, it runs close to the upper third of the anterior vaginal wall (Jackson, 2019; Rahn, 2007).

570
01:06:39,361 --> 01:06:48,724
Finally, the ureter enters the bladder and travels obliquely for approximately 1.5 cm before opening at the ureteral orifices.

571
01:06:48,724 --> 01:06:58,286
The pelvic ureter receives blood supply from the vessels it passes: the common iliac, internal iliac, uterine, and superior vesical vessels.

572
01:06:58,286 --> 01:07:05,786
The ureter’s course runs medial to these vessels, and thus its blood supply reaches the ureter from lateral sources.

573
01:07:05,786 --> 01:07:09,261
This is important during ureteral isolation.

574
01:07:09,261 --> 01:07:16,486
Vascular anastomoses on the connective tissue sheath enveloping the ureter form a longitudinal network of vessels.

575
01:07:16,486 --> 01:07:18,924
Pelvic Skeletal Anatomy.

576
01:07:18,924 --> 01:07:21,836
# Pelvic Bones and Joints.

577
01:07:21,836 --> 01:07:28,061
The pelvis is composed of four bones—the sacrum, coccyx, and two innominate bones.

578
01:07:28,061 --> 01:07:34,424
Each innominate bone is formed by the fusion of three bones—the ilium, ischium, and pubis .

579
01:07:34,424 --> 01:07:39,136
Both innominate bones are joined to the sacrum at the sacroiliac joint.

580
01:07:39,136 --> 01:07:43,099
Anteriorly, they are joined at the symphysis pubis.

581
01:07:43,099 --> 01:07:48,611
This consists of fibrocartilage and the superior and inferior pubic ligaments.

582
01:07:48,611 --> 01:07:53,811
The latter ligament is frequently designated the arcuate ligament of the pubis.

583
01:07:53,811 --> 01:07:56,261
Figure 2-14.

584
01:07:56,261 --> 01:08:02,711
The innominate bone is composed of the pubis (brown), ischium (red), and ilium (blue).

585
01:08:02,711 --> 01:08:10,386
Of the three anteroposterior diameters of the pelvic inlet, only the diagonal conjugate can be measured clinically.

586
01:08:10,386 --> 01:08:17,811
The important obstetrical conjugate is derived by subtracting 1.5 cm from the diagonal conjugate.

587
01:08:17,811 --> 01:08:23,961
These pelvic joints have a limited degree of mobility but can relax remarkably during pregnancy.

588
01:08:23,961 --> 01:08:28,724
For example, at term, the sacroiliac joint can glide upward.

589
01:08:28,724 --> 01:08:37,986
This is greatest in dorsal lithotomy position and may increase the diameter of the outlet by 1.5 to 2.0 cm for delivery .

590
01:08:37,986 --> 01:08:46,486
Sacroiliac joint mobility also likely aids the McRoberts maneuver to release an obstructed shoulder in cases of shoulder dystocia .

591
01:08:46,486 --> 01:08:53,574
These changes may also contribute to the success of the modified squatting position to hasten second-stage labor .

592
01:08:53,574 --> 01:09:02,024
Squatting may increase the interspinous diameter and the pelvic outlet diameter (Russell, 1969, 1982).

593
01:09:02,024 --> 01:09:05,499
# Planes and Diameters of the Pelvis.

594
01:09:05,499 --> 01:09:10,099
The pelvis is conceptually divided into false and true components.

595
01:09:10,099 --> 01:09:16,274
The false pelvis lies above the linea terminalis, and the true pelvis is below this boundary .

596
01:09:16,274 --> 01:09:22,624
The false pelvis is bounded posteriorly by the lumbar vertebra and laterally by the iliac fossa.

597
01:09:22,624 --> 01:09:28,449
In front, the boundary is formed by the lower portion of the anterior abdominal wall.

598
01:09:28,449 --> 01:09:30,811
Figure 2-15.

599
01:09:30,811 --> 01:09:34,086
Axial view of a normal female pelvis.

600
01:09:34,086 --> 01:09:40,661
The clinically important obstetrical conjugate and transverse diameter of the pelvic inlet are illustrated.

601
01:09:40,661 --> 01:09:44,999
The interspinous diameter of the midpelvis also is marked.

602
01:09:44,999 --> 01:09:52,749
The true pelvis is described by four imaginary planes: 1. The plane of the pelvic inlet—the superior strait.

603
01:09:52,749 --> 01:09:57,024
2. The plane of the pelvic outlet—the inferior strait.

604
01:09:57,024 --> 01:10:01,511
3. The plane of the midpelvis—the least pelvic dimensions.

605
01:10:01,511 --> 01:10:07,036
4. The plane of greatest pelvic dimension—of no obstetrical significance.

606
01:10:07,036 --> 01:10:18,774
Pelvic Inlet The pelvic inlet is bounded posteriorly by the promontory, laterally by the linea terminalis, and anteriorly by the horizontal pubic rami and the symphysis pubis.

607
01:10:18,774 --> 01:10:26,311
During labor, fetal head engagement is defined by the fetal head’s biparietal diameter passing through this plane.

608
01:10:26,311 --> 01:10:34,511
Four diameters of the pelvic inlet are usually described: anteroposterior, transverse, and two oblique diameters.

609
01:10:34,511 --> 01:10:40,999
Of these, distinct anteroposterior diameters have been described using specific landmarks.

610
01:10:40,999 --> 01:10:52,961
Most cephalad, the anteroposterior diameter, termed the true conjugate, extends from the uppermost margin of the symphysis pubis to the sacral promontory (see Fig. 2-14).

611
01:10:52,961 --> 01:11:02,311
The clinically important obstetrical conjugate is the shortest, and thus the most limiting, distance between the sacral promontory and the symphysis pubis.

612
01:11:02,311 --> 01:11:10,586
Normally, this measures 10 cm or more, but unfortunately, it cannot be measured directly with examining fingers.

613
01:11:10,586 --> 01:11:19,199
Thus, the obstetrical conjugate is estimated indirectly by subtracting 1.5 to 2 cm from the diagonal conjugate.

614
01:11:19,199 --> 01:11:22,449
To measure the diagonal conjugate, a

615
01:11:22,612 --> 01:11:27,949
hand with the palm oriented laterally extends its index finger to the promontory.

616
01:11:27,899 --> 01:11:35,974
The distance from the fingertip to the point at which the lowest margin of the symphysis strikes the same finger’s base is the diagonal conjugate.

617
01:11:35,974 --> 01:11:47,537
The transverse diameter is constructed at right angles to the obstetrical conjugate and represents the greatest distance between the linea terminalis on either side (see Fig. 2-15).

618
01:11:47,537 --> 01:11:57,474
It usually intersects the obstetrical conjugate at a point approximately 5 cm in front of the promontory and measures approximately 13 cm.

619
01:11:57,474 --> 01:12:08,612
Midpelvis and Pelvic Outlet The midpelvis is measured at the level of the ischial spines, also called the midplane or plane of least pelvic dimensions (see Fig. 2-15).

620
01:12:08,612 --> 01:12:18,649
During labor, the degree of fetal head descent into the true pelvis may be described by station, and the midpelvis and ischial spines serve to mark zero station.

621
01:12:18,649 --> 01:12:26,374
The interspinous diameter is 10 cm or slightly greater and is usually the smallest overall pelvic diameter.

622
01:12:26,374 --> 01:12:34,362
The anteroposterior diameter through the level of the ischial spines normally measures at least 11.5 cm.

623
01:12:34,362 --> 01:12:43,549
The pelvic outlet consists of two approximately triangular areas whose boundaries mirror those of the perineum described earlier (p. 14).

624
01:12:43,549 --> 01:12:49,162
They have a common base, which is a line drawn between the two ischial tuberosities.

625
01:12:49,162 --> 01:12:58,387
The apex of the posterior triangle is the tip of the sacrum, and the lateral boundaries are the sacrotuberous ligaments and the ischial tuberosities.

626
01:12:58,387 --> 01:13:04,137
The anterior triangle is formed by the descending inferior rami of the pubic bones.

627
01:13:04,137 --> 01:13:11,637
These rami unite at an angle of 90 to 100 degrees to form a rounded arch under which the fetal head must pass.

628
01:13:11,637 --> 01:13:18,424
Unless there is significant pelvic bony disease, the pelvic outlet seldom obstructs vaginal delivery.

629
01:13:18,424 --> 01:13:20,887
# Pelvic Shapes.

630
01:13:20,887 --> 01:13:32,649
The Caldwell–Moloy (1933, 1934) anatomical classification of the pelvis is based on shape, and its concepts aid an understanding of labor mechanisms.

631
01:13:32,649 --> 01:13:45,587
Specifically, the greatest transverse diameter of the inlet and its division into anterior and posterior segments are used to classify the pelvis as gynecoid, anthropoid, android, or platypelloid.

632
01:13:45,587 --> 01:13:52,574
The posterior segment determines the type of pelvis, whereas the anterior segment determines the tendency.

633
01:13:52,574 --> 01:13:57,724
These are both determined because many pelves are not pure but are mixed types.

634
01:13:57,724 --> 01:14:07,087
For example, a gynecoid pelvis with an android tendency means that the posterior pelvis is gynecoid and the anterior pelvis is android shaped.

635
01:14:07,087 --> 01:14:17,124
From viewing the four basic types in Figure 2-16, the configuration of the gynecoid pelvis would intuitively seem suited for delivery of most fetuses.

636
01:14:17,124 --> 01:14:24,337
Indeed, Caldwell (1939) reported that the gynecoid pelvis was found in almost half of women.

637
01:14:24,337 --> 01:14:26,799
Figure 2-16.

638
01:14:26,799 --> 01:14:34,024
The four parent pelvic types of the Caldwell–Moloy classification are shown by anatomic and schematic drawings.

639
01:14:34,024 --> 01:14:44,362
In each, the blue line passes through the widest transverse diameter and divides the pelvic inlets into posterior (P) and anterior (A) segments.

640
01:14:44,362 --> 01:14:51,024
Mixed types are formed by combining anterior and posterior inlet segments from different parent types.

